      "text": "Mimi Audio Codec",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Mimi Audio Codec",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "created": 1790085095079,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "RKDWYXe-8dLenkNwP6dIm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4962.587107340495,
      "y": 1321.6040776388909,
      "width": 890.8257853190103,
      "height": 1825,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "VGfWA4PooMGpcXYj-LvoD",
        "51y54vqDRhwjgdT4NzjcC",
        "-O6C1wlguyIMlI181LzqI"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 926011115,
      "version": 5755,
      "versionNonce": 1114884233,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790271301445,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "AudioLM is not a good fit for real time TTS. The main obstacle is its hierarchical\ngeneration schedule.\n\n\n                                        Stage 1\n                                        Generate semantic tokens\n                                        for the whole sequence\n                                                ↓\n                                        Stage 2\n                                        Generate coarse acoustic tokens\n                                        conditioned on semantic tokens\n                                                ↓\n                                        Stage 3\n                                        Generate fine acoustic tokens\n                                        conditioned on coarse tokens\n                                                ↓\n                                        SoundStream decoder\n                                                ↓\n                                        Audio\n\n\nmodels the semantic tokens for the entire sequence\n\nMimi converts audio into the discrete tokens predicted by Moshi and back, using residual\nvector \nquantization (RVQ).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFor real time dialogue system also the tokenizer has to generate semqntic and audio\ntokens  \"on the fly\".\nProblem: recall that the standard (AudioLM) tokenization approach works like this\n    Audio → Semantic encoder → Semantic tokens\n    Audio → Acoustic encoder → Acoustic tokens\nSemantic tokens are NOT causal => they require the entire audio to compute (HuBERT,\nWavLM, \nw2v-BERT are all based on full-context transformers) hence can only be computed offline.\n\n(Also having two separate encoders is inefficient)\n\nMimi uses one encoder, 8 (1 semantic and 7 acustic) quantizers, each with a codebook size\nof \nNA = 2048. The innovationis that they train the first level of Mimi’s RVQ to imitate\nWavLM \nembeddings.\n    audio → WavLM → semantic_embeddings(t)\n    audio -> Mimi encoder -> z(t) -> VQ(z(t))\nThey then compute a cosine distance between the output of the first quantizer and the \ntransformed WavLM embeddings, to perform distillation. They are not teaching Mimi to\nreproduce \n“whatever WavLM would output causally on the same partial chunk.” They are teaching Mimi\nto \napproximate the output of a stronger, non-causal teacher representation computed with\nfuture \ncontext available during training.\n\nThe architecture takes inspiration from SoundStream, and improves its ability to encode\nspeech \ninto dense representations with Transformer modules in the bottleneck.\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "AudioLM is not a good fit for real time TTS. The main obstacle is its hierarchical generation schedule.\n\n\n                                        Stage 1\n                                        Generate semantic tokens\n                                        for the whole sequence\n                                                ↓\n                                        Stage 2\n                                        Generate coarse acoustic tokens\n                                        conditioned on semantic tokens\n                                                ↓\n                                        Stage 3\n                                        Generate fine acoustic tokens\n                                        conditioned on coarse tokens\n                                                ↓\n                                        SoundStream decoder\n                                                ↓\n                                        Audio\n\n\nmodels the semantic tokens for the entire sequence\n\nMimi converts audio into the discrete tokens predicted by Moshi and back, using residual vector \nquantization (RVQ).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFor real time dialogue system also the tokenizer has to generate semqntic and audio tokens  \"on the fly\".\nProblem: recall that the standard (AudioLM) tokenization approach works like this\n    Audio → Semantic encoder → Semantic tokens\n    Audio → Acoustic encoder → Acoustic tokens\nSemantic tokens are NOT causal => they require the entire audio to compute (HuBERT, WavLM, \nw2v-BERT are all based on full-context transformers) hence can only be computed offline.\n\n(Also having two separate encoders is inefficient)\n\nMimi uses one encoder, 8 (1 semantic and 7 acustic) quantizers, each with a codebook size of \nNA = 2048. The innovationis that they train the first level of Mimi’s RVQ to imitate WavLM \nembeddings.\n    audio → WavLM → semantic_embeddings(t)\n    audio -> Mimi encoder -> z(t) -> VQ(z(t))\nThey then compute a cosine distance between the output of the first quantizer and the \ntransformed WavLM embeddings, to perform distillation. They are not teaching Mimi to reproduce \n“whatever WavLM would output causally on the same partial chunk.” They are teaching Mimi to \napproximate the output of a stronger, non-causal teacher representation computed with future \ncontext available during training.\n\nThe architecture takes inspiration from SoundStream, and improves its ability to encode speech \ninto dense representations with Transformer modules in the bottleneck.\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790085095079,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
