    {
      "id": "i5RxpXJsBMM8hEzU4gW7N",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6618.198766532166,
      "y": 60.9403049085804,
      "width": 981.4190673828125,
      "height": 2325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "cn4g0289h9SDG5J6zzBsT"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 750826663,
      "version": 4864,
      "versionNonce": 367983399,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790271280398,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The main components of Moshi are: \n    - a text language model backbone (Helium)\n    - a neural audio codec model (Mimi)\n    - hierarchical generation of semantic and acoustic\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nMoshi introduces the first multi-stream audio language model, i.e. a model that explicitly processes \nthe input and output audio streams jointly. \n    --> This removes the concept of speaker turn but train the model on natural conversations with \narbitrary dynamics including overlap and interruptions. \n\nMoshi always listens and always generates sound, either speech or silence\n\nMIMI A NEW NEURAL AUDIO CODEC\nMimi converts audio into the discrete tokens predicted by Moshi and back, using residual vector \nquantization (RVQ).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFor real time dialogue system also the tokenizer has to generate semqntic and audio tokens \n\"on the fly\".\nProblem: recall that the standard (AudioLM) tokenization approach works like this\n    Audio → Semantic encoder → Semantic tokens\n    Audio → Acoustic encoder → Acoustic tokens\nSemantic tokens are NOT causal => they require the entire audio to compute (HuBERT, WavLM, \nw2v-BERT are all based on full-context transformers) hence can only be computed offline.\n\n(Also having two separate encoders is inefficient)\n\nMimi uses one encoder, 8 (1 semantic and 7 acustic) quantizers, each with a codebook size of \nNA = 2048. The innovationis that they train the first level of Mimi’s RVQ to imitate WavLM \nembeddings.\n    audio → WavLM → semantic_embeddings(t)\n    audio -> Mimi encoder -> z(t) -> VQ(z(t))\nThey then compute a cosine distance between the output of the first quantizer and the \ntransformed WavLM embeddings, to perform distillation. They are not teaching Mimi to reproduce \n“whatever WavLM would output causally on the same partial chunk.” They are teaching Mimi to \napproximate the output of a stronger, non-causal teacher representation computed with future \ncontext available during training.\n\nThe architecture takes inspiration from SoundStream, and improves its ability to encode speech \ninto dense representations with Transformer modules in the bottleneck.\n\nJOINT REPRESENTATION OF SEQUENCES\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEach column represents the tokens for a given step in the joint sequence.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The main components of Moshi are: \n    - a text language model backbone (Helium)\n    - a neural audio codec model (Mimi)\n    - hierarchical generation of semantic and acoustic\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nMoshi introduces the first multi-stream audio language model, i.e. a model that explicitly processes \nthe input and output audio streams jointly. \n    --> This removes the concept of speaker turn but train the model on natural conversations with \narbitrary dynamics including overlap and interruptions. \n\nMoshi always listens and always generates sound, either speech or silence\n\nMIMI A NEW NEURAL AUDIO CODEC\nMimi converts audio into the discrete tokens predicted by Moshi and back, using residual vector \nquantization (RVQ).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFor real time dialogue system also the tokenizer has to generate semqntic and audio tokens \n\"on the fly\".\nProblem: recall that the standard (AudioLM) tokenization approach works like this\n    Audio → Semantic encoder → Semantic tokens\n    Audio → Acoustic encoder → Acoustic tokens\nSemantic tokens are NOT causal => they require the entire audio to compute (HuBERT, WavLM, \nw2v-BERT are all based on full-context transformers) hence can only be computed offline.\n\n(Also having two separate encoders is inefficient)\n\nMimi uses one encoder, 8 (1 semantic and 7 acustic) quantizers, each with a codebook size of \nNA = 2048. The innovationis that they train the first level of Mimi’s RVQ to imitate WavLM \nembeddings.\n    audio → WavLM → semantic_embeddings(t)\n    audio -> Mimi encoder -> z(t) -> VQ(z(t))\nThey then compute a cosine distance between the output of the first quantizer and the \ntransformed WavLM embeddings, to perform distillation. They are not teaching Mimi to reproduce \n“whatever WavLM would output causally on the same partial chunk.” They are teaching Mimi to \napproximate the output of a stronger, non-causal teacher representation computed with future \ncontext available during training.\n\nThe architecture takes inspiration from SoundStream, and improves its ability to encode speech \ninto dense representations with Transformer modules in the bottleneck.\n\nJOINT REPRESENTATION OF SEQUENCES\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEach column represents the tokens for a given step in the joint sequence.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ETibppHsFAPfrrFHOZkOG",
      "type": "image",
      "x": 6668.288396365063,
      "y": 866.4932676753315,
      "width": 813.3865791393168,
      "height": 283.10561056105695,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "transparent",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
