      "seed": 1566072807,
      "version": 4171,
      "versionNonce": 1539973831,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790271280398,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Hierarchical Transformer",
      "fontSize": 39.54285714285655,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Hierarchical Transformer",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "9jjpAtPkF-sInJ-sQHUr7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6627.80751221308,
      "y": 2613.539609860899,
      "width": 1000.3790283203125,
      "height": 1175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1127336711,
      "version": 5105,
      "versionNonce": 930320681,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790271280398,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When dealing with streaming generating Q = 8 codebooks for each frame starts being a real\nproblem.\nAt a frame rate of 12.5hz, 100 (12.5*8) autoregressive steps are needed for just one second \nof audio. Generating 100 tokens per second is unfeasable in most case scenarious. \nAs a comparison, text can be represented with around 3 to 4 tokens per second.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe RQ-Transformer consists in two Transformer models,\n    - a larger Temporal Transformer, received the aggregated (sum) representation of the [K, S] \n        input sequence. This means it receves a sequence of S timesteps. The Temporal Transformer\n        produces a single context embedding\n    - a smaller Depth Transformer, receives as input the sum of the context embedding and of a \n        learnt embedding and autoregressively generate the model audio tokens in K steps.\n\nCompared with AudioLM which first generates all the semantic tokens, Moshi generate them jointly \nwith the acoustic tokens, which allows for streaming modeling of semantic and acoustic tokens jointly.\n\nThe context embedding from Temporal Transformer is also used to represent the token of the inner \ndialogue. Giving to Moshi the textual representation of its own speech improves the linguistic quality \nof its generation.\n\nNote! no textual representation from the stream of the user is used. This would require an external \nASR and break the end-to-end approach.\n\nWasn't helium a text model? Yes, at moshi they trained a text LLM from scratch.\nThe training of Moshi goes through 4 phases: \n    1. Pre-training on unsupervised data (with Temporal Transformer initialized from Helium) and \n        single stream of audio data; \n    2. Post-training with simulated multi-stream based on diarization; \n    3. Fine-tuning on the Fisher dataset (real multi-stream interactions) to gain its fully duplex \n        capabilities; \n    4. Instruction fine-tuning on a custom dataset built from synthetic interaction scripts.\n\nMoshi is trained to model joint sequences. Given the ground-truth discrete text, semantic and \nacustic token.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When dealing with streaming generating Q = 8 codebooks for each frame starts being a real\nproblem.\nAt a frame rate of 12.5hz, 100 (12.5*8) autoregressive steps are needed for just one second \nof audio. Generating 100 tokens per second is unfeasable in most case scenarious. \nAs a comparison, text can be represented with around 3 to 4 tokens per second.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe RQ-Transformer consists in two Transformer models,\n    - a larger Temporal Transformer, received the aggregated (sum) representation of the [K, S] \n        input sequence. This means it receves a sequence of S timesteps. The Temporal Transformer\n        produces a single context embedding\n    - a smaller Depth Transformer, receives as input the sum of the context embedding and of a \n        learnt embedding and autoregressively generate the model audio tokens in K steps.\n\nCompared with AudioLM which first generates all the semantic tokens, Moshi generate them jointly \nwith the acoustic tokens, which allows for streaming modeling of semantic and acoustic tokens jointly.\n\nThe context embedding from Temporal Transformer is also used to represent the token of the inner \ndialogue. Giving to Moshi the textual representation of its own speech improves the linguistic quality \nof its generation.\n\nNote! no textual representation from the stream of the user is used. This would require an external \nASR and break the end-to-end approach.\n\nWasn't helium a text model? Yes, at moshi they trained a text LLM from scratch.\nThe training of Moshi goes through 4 phases: \n    1. Pre-training on unsupervised data (with Temporal Transformer initialized from Helium) and \n        single stream of audio data; \n    2. Post-training with simulated multi-stream based on diarization; \n    3. Fine-tuning on the Fisher dataset (real multi-stream interactions) to gain its fully duplex \n        capabilities; \n    4. Instruction fine-tuning on a custom dataset built from synthetic interaction scripts.\n\nMoshi is trained to model joint sequences. Given the ground-truth discrete text, semantic and \nacustic token.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
