      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Interleaved Spoken and Written Language Model",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Interleaved Spoken and Written Language Model",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.15
    },
    {
      "id": "02ILTRzWdnaOSveT9HktY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 975.4844341505141,
      "y": 786.5920412245332,
      "width": 910.0891723632812,
      "height": 2575,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "v7JSLsv4Saz2GrvKnf3Vz",
        "VtGVZ6hzYuOasINpcvm3n",
        "pR5BmR_Z_hpOshAp1joMm",
        "y8tkQwi6ABTGpgZM_SZCQ",
        "JkqWKUNGVtc5dnEsoEfw3",
        "eReCPuTb3c416PsjyUawy"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1qV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1583253577,
      "version": 4891,
      "versionNonce": 1934731463,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790333985356,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "In a cascade architecture the intermediate text representation removes important\ninformation contained in human speech, such as:\n- intonation,\n- speaking style,\n- rhythm,\n- emotion,\n- emphasis.\n\nAlso text models are already very good at what they do!\n\nHow can we train a text language model to understand and generate speech and text\ntogether?\n\nIn SPIRIT LM:\n- text is represented using standard subword tokens (BPE tokens);\n- speech is represented using units extracted from models such as HuBERT.\n\nThe sees sequences such as:\n\n                             \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe model's task becomes predicting the next token, regardless of whether that token\nrepresents written language or spoken language.\n\nThe most interesting idea introduced by SPIRIT LM is interleaving.\n\nInstead of training only with independent speech and text examples, the model receives\nsequences where the two modalities are mixed together. For a sentence like \"The cat sat on\nthe mat\":\n\n  [TEXT] The cat [SPEECH] (audio tokens corresponding to \"sat on\") [TEXT] the mat...\n\nThe authors analyze the internal representations (hidden states) of the model when it\nreceives the same sentence in two modalities:\n    - written text: \"timothy saw the gray mouse quite plainly\"\n    - spoken version of the same sentence.\n\nThey measure how similar the internal representations of speech and text become across\nthe different layers of the transformer.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBy the middle layers, the model is no longer treating speech and text as completely different\nsignals. It has learned: \"This sequence of HuBERT speech tokens and this sequence of BPE\ntext tokens describe the same linguistic concept.\"\n\nThe blue line is the control experiment. This model receives both speech and text during\ntraining, but without mixing them together. \n    The model may know speech.\n    The model may know text.\n    But the two representations remain separated.\n\nThe yellow line is the intermediate case. Here, speech and text are paired at the word level:\n\n                                    [TEXT] the\n                                    [SPEECH] corresponding audio to \"cat\"\n                                    [TEXT] sat\n                                    [SPEECH] corresponding audio to \"on\"\n                                    ...\n\nThe model learns some alignment, but less effectively.\n\n\nThe authors showed that the same multimodal language model can perform ASR and TTS\ntasks through in context learning.\n\nFor ASR hey prompted the model with examples of speech-text pairs:\n\n                        [SPEECH] speech tokens → [TEXT] transcription\n\nThen, given a new speech input:\n\n                                    [SPEECH] [Hu34][Hu71][Hu405]...\n\nthe model generates:\n\n                                    [TEXT] Yellowstone national park is...\n\nFor TTS they reverse process.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "In a cascade architecture the intermediate text representation removes important information contained in human speech, such as:\n- intonation,\n- speaking style,\n- rhythm,\n- emotion,\n- emphasis.\n\nAlso text models are already very good at what they do!\n\nHow can we train a text language model to understand and generate speech and text together?\n\nIn SPIRIT LM:\n- text is represented using standard subword tokens (BPE tokens);\n- speech is represented using units extracted from models such as HuBERT.\n\nThe sees sequences such as:\n\n                             \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe model's task becomes predicting the next token, regardless of whether that token represents written language or spoken language.\n\nThe most interesting idea introduced by SPIRIT LM is interleaving.\n\nInstead of training only with independent speech and text examples, the model receives sequences where the two modalities are mixed together. For a sentence like \"The cat sat on the mat\":\n\n  [TEXT] The cat [SPEECH] (audio tokens corresponding to \"sat on\") [TEXT] the mat...\n\nThe authors analyze the internal representations (hidden states) of the model when it receives the same sentence in two modalities:\n    - written text: \"timothy saw the gray mouse quite plainly\"\n    - spoken version of the same sentence.\n\nThey measure how similar the internal representations of speech and text become across the different layers of the transformer.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBy the middle layers, the model is no longer treating speech and text as completely different signals. It has learned: \"This sequence of HuBERT speech tokens and this sequence of BPE text tokens describe the same linguistic concept.\"\n\nThe blue line is the control experiment. This model receives both speech and text during training, but without mixing them together. \n    The model may know speech.\n    The model may know text.\n    But the two representations remain separated.\n\nThe yellow line is the intermediate case. Here, speech and text are paired at the word level:\n\n                                    [TEXT] the\n                                    [SPEECH] corresponding audio to \"cat\"\n                                    [TEXT] sat\n                                    [SPEECH] corresponding audio to \"on\"\n                                    ...\n\nThe model learns some alignment, but less effectively.\n\n\nThe authors showed that the same multimodal language model can perform ASR and TTS tasks through in context learning.\n\nFor ASR hey prompted the model with examples of speech-text pairs:\n\n                        [SPEECH] speech tokens → [TEXT] transcription\n\nThen, given a new speech input:\n\n                                    [SPEECH] [Hu34][Hu71][Hu405]...\n\nthe model generates:\n\n                                    [TEXT] Yellowstone national park is...\n\nFor TTS they reverse process.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
